📗
  Protocol for numbers, which provide arithmetic and comparisons.

  🔢, 💯 and 💧 conform to 📈 with themselves as T. Generic code can use a
  generic parameter constrained to 📈 of itself, like `T 📈🐚T🍆`, to compute
  with any of them:

  ```
  🐇❗️ 🧃🐚T 📈🐚T🍆🍆 a T b T ➡️ T 🍇
    ↩️ a ✖️ a ➕ b ✖️ b
  🍉
  ```

  Such code is specialized for each type it is used with, so that it computes
  as fast as code written for that type.
📗
🌍 🐊 📈🐚T⚪🍆 🍇
  📗 Returns the receiver plus *other*. 📗
  ➕ other T ➡️ T
  📗 Returns the receiver minus *other*. 📗
  ➖ other T ➡️ T
  📗 Returns the receiver multiplied by *other*. 📗
  ✖️ other T ➡️ T
  📗 Returns the receiver divided by *other*. 📗
  ➗ other T ➡️ T
  📗 Returns the receiver modulus *other*. 📗
  🚮 other T ➡️ T
  📗 Returns true if the receiver is smaller than *other*. 📗
  ◀️ other T ➡️ 👌
  📗 Returns true if the receiver is greater than *other*. 📗
  ▶️ other T ➡️ 👌
  📗 Returns true if the receiver is smaller than or equal to *other*. 📗
  ◀️🙌 other T ➡️ 👌
  📗 Returns true if the receiver is greater than or equal to *other*. 📗
  ▶️🙌 other T ➡️ 👌
🍉
